faq:
  - q: "What are the three main resume formats?"
    a: "Chronological (most common — lists experience newest to oldest, best for linear career paths and ATS), Functional (groups by skill rather than timeline — often penalized by ATS and viewed with suspicion by recruiters), Hybrid/Combination (chronological structure with a prominent skills section — best for most professionals at $100K+ levels)."
  - q: "Which resume format is best for ATS?"
    a: "Chronological is the most ATS-compatible format. Functional resumes frequently fail ATS parsing because they obscure employment dates and break the expected data structure. Hybrid resumes are also generally ATS-safe if they avoid columns and tables."
  - q: "Which format do recruiters prefer?"
    a: "Chronological or hybrid. Functional resumes raise red flags — recruiters associate them with candidates trying to hide employment gaps or limited relevant experience. Unless you have a specific structural reason to use functional, default to chronological or hybrid."
  - q: "Should I use a chronological or hybrid resume?"
    a: "For most professionals at $100K+ levels with 5+ years of experience: hybrid. It gives ATS the clean chronological structure it expects while adding a visible Skills section that optimizes keyword matching. Chronological works best when your titles and employers are strong signals on their own."
  - q: "Can I use a functional resume if I have employment gaps?"
    a: "No — use a chronological resume with an honest explanation in your cover letter or early interview. Functional resumes do not hide gaps; they flag them. Recruiters see the missing dates immediately and are more suspicious of a hidden gap than a disclosed one."
